1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,799
This is the Black Ant plan for moving the Earth.

2
00:00:04,949 --> 00:00:15,077
The goal: carry Earth from its orbit at 1 AU out to 2.7 AU, near the asteroid belt.

3
00:00:15,227 --> 00:00:22,080
It must get there within 10,000 years, without harming the planet or anyone on it.

4
00:00:23,080 --> 00:00:29,723
Rule 1, Newton's third law: every push comes with an equal push back.

5
00:00:29,873 --> 00:00:38,522
Sit in a car and push its dashboard: the car does not move. Your push and the push back cancel.

6
00:00:38,672 --> 00:00:47,725
A machine standing on Earth, pushing against Earth, is the same: it only pushes Earth against itself.

7
00:00:47,875 --> 00:00:52,564
To move a planet, the force must come from outside it.

8
00:00:53,564 --> 00:01:01,713
Rule 2, gravity: every mass pulls on every other mass, even across empty space.

9
00:01:01,863 --> 00:01:09,276
The pull weakens with the square of the distance: twice as far, a quarter of the pull.

10
00:01:09,426 --> 00:01:16,045
No rope, and no contact. Just mass, pulling on mass.

11
00:01:17,045 --> 00:01:24,079
So we use a rock: an asteroid about 670 km wide.

12
00:01:24,229 --> 00:01:32,817
It is parked 637,100 km from Earth, 1.7 times as far as the Moon.

13
00:01:32,967 --> 00:01:38,346
It sits slightly ahead of Earth, and slightly further from the Sun.

14
00:01:38,496 --> 00:01:43,499
Its gravity gently pulls Earth forward, along its path.

15
00:01:44,499 --> 00:01:50,143
Rule 3: a push along the direction of motion makes an orbit bigger.

16
00:01:50,293 --> 00:01:56,332
Every lap around the Sun, Earth's orbit widens, just a little.

17
00:01:56,482 --> 00:02:03,130
The pull is tiny: 6.5 × 10⁻⁸ m/s², under a ten-millionth of a metre per second, every second.

18
00:02:03,280 --> 00:02:11,204
But it never stops: over 8,700 years it carries Earth all the way out.

19
00:02:12,204 --> 00:02:19,537
But gravity works both ways: Earth pulls the rock just as hard as the rock pulls Earth.

20
00:02:19,687 --> 00:02:26,771
Left alone, the rock would fall onto Earth. So it has to be held in place.

21
00:02:27,771 --> 00:02:37,864
Rule 4, light pushes: light carries momentum, so when it bounces off a mirror it gives the mirror a small push.

22
00:02:38,014 --> 00:02:46,497
A huge mirror, made of a super-strong material (our assumption E2), is tied to the rock with cables.

23
00:02:46,647 --> 00:02:58,275
Far away on the Sun's side, many light stations shine a beam onto it: our assumption E1, up to 10²⁶ W of light.

24
00:02:58,425 --> 00:03:08,194
The bounced light pushes the rock away from Earth exactly as hard as Earth pulls it back, so the rock hovers on its spot.

25
00:03:08,344 --> 00:03:16,192
The beam never comes closer to Earth than 420,000 km, further out than the Moon.

26
00:03:17,192 --> 00:03:27,940
Mars lies across our path, so a second, smaller rock tows Mars out first, to 3.64 AU.

27
00:03:28,090 --> 00:03:35,913
On the way, Jupiter's gravity tries to trap Mars at a resonance; the tow sprints across it.

28
00:03:36,063 --> 00:03:43,177
Mars is parked by year 5,168, long before Earth arrives.

29
00:03:44,177 --> 00:03:53,835
Rule 5, energy balance: a planet's temperature is set by the sunlight it absorbs and the heat it radiates away.

30
00:03:53,985 --> 00:04:04,473
At 2.7 AU sunlight is 7.3 times weaker: Earth would freeze at about 150 K.

31
00:04:04,623 --> 00:04:11,386
So mirrors in space (our assumption E3) send extra sunlight to Earth.

32
00:04:11,536 --> 00:04:21,384
Earth always gets today's sunlight and stays near 288 K, with its water, air and Moon kept safe.

33
00:04:22,384 --> 00:04:30,038
The asteroid belt is mostly empty space: its big rocks are millions of kilometres apart.

34
00:04:30,188 --> 00:04:38,176
Still, without help, a 1 km asteroid would hit Earth about once every 130 years.

35
00:04:38,326 --> 00:04:44,805
So each dangerous one is spotted 10 years ahead and nudged aside by a weak beam.

36
00:04:45,805 --> 00:04:53,028
Rule 6, conservation: momentum and energy are never created from nothing.

37
00:04:53,178 --> 00:05:04,206
Our simulation books every push with its partner: the light pushes the rock, the stations recoil, the bounced light carries momentum away.

38
00:05:04,356 --> 00:05:11,674
The books balance to better than one part in 10¹⁵ (5.6 × 10⁻¹⁶); the limit is one part in a billion.

39
00:05:12,674 --> 00:05:20,388
In year 8,758, Earth arrives at 2.7 AU.

40
00:05:20,538 --> 00:05:24,732
The beams switch off, and the rock is sent away.

41
00:05:24,882 --> 00:05:31,795
We then ran the new solar system for a million years: Earth stays in its new orbit.

42
00:05:31,945 --> 00:05:38,588
That is how a light-held gravity tractor moves a planet. Team Black Ant.

